(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-1-abnormal-cell-growth-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Abnormal Cell Growth and Differentiation Quiz 2 — Clin Path I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is cancer, and where does the word come from?","choices":["A malignant neoplasm; from the Latin for crab","Any neoplasm at all; from the Latin for growth, because the mass enlarges without a stimulus","A neoplasm that has metastasized; from the Greek for wandering, because cells migrate","A precancerous lesion; from the Latin for disorder, because architecture is lost"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — so named because it adheres to any tissue it seizes upon and reaches out with claws, invading surrounding tissue. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which inherited tumour suppressor alteration is associated with malignant melanoma?","choices":["Adenomatous polyposis coli","BRCA-1","p16, also called INK4a","Retinoblastoma protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — one of five hereditary examples the deck lists. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What does the degree of differentiation of a neoplasm measure?","choices":["How many of its cells are in the proliferative pool at any moment","How closely it resembles comparable normal cells in appearance and function","How far it has spread from the primary site within the patient","How rapidly the tumour mass is enlarging over time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — this is the basis of histological grading. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does the deck say about differentiation in malignant tumours?","choices":["Always poorly differentiated","Always anaplastic","Always well differentiated","Various degrees of differentiation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — malignancy does not mean uniformly poor differentiation. Benign tumours are usually well differentiated. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which cancers is Epstein Barr virus associated with, and which cells does it infect?","choices":["Gastric adenocarcinoma and mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue lymphoma; it colonizes the stomach","B cell lymphomas and nasopharyngeal carcinoma; it infects B lymphocytes","Hepatocellular carcinoma; it infects hepatocytes and causes chronic injury with regeneration","Cervical carcinoma; it infects cervical epithelium and integrates its genome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it immortalizes them, which is the key step, and it also infects epithelial cells of the oropharynx. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which tumours are associated with an inherited alteration of the retinoblastoma protein?","choices":["Neurofibromatosis types 1 and 2, with tumours of the central and peripheral nervous system","Malignant melanoma","Familial adenomatous polyposis and colon cancer","Retinoblastoma, a rare childhood tumour of the eye, and osteosarcoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the same tumour suppressor, two very different tumours. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which clinical example did Professor Rappa give for metaplasia, and what changes into what?","choices":["Barrett's oesophagus — squamous becomes columnar under chronic acid reflux","Barrett's oesophagus — columnar becomes squamous under chronic acid reflux","Cervical dysplasia — squamous becomes columnar under human papillomavirus infection","Endometrial hyperplasia — glandular becomes squamous under excess oestrogen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the lower oesophagus is squamous and cannot resist acid, so chronic reflux drives the change. — 2026-08-20 lecture recording, 7:45"},{"q":"What does falling differentiation mean for the behaviour of a tumour, according to Professor Rappa?","choices":["It becomes more aggressive","It becomes less aggressive, because poorly differentiated cells divide more slowly","Aggressiveness is unrelated to differentiation and depends only on stage","It becomes more aggressive only once the basement membrane has been breached"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — an anaplastic carcinoma is very aggressive compared with a well differentiated one. The deck gives grading as a scale of resemblance and stops there; he attaches the prognosis to it. — 2026-08-20 lecture recording, 24:08"},{"q":"Besides cervical cancer, which malignancies is high-risk human papillomavirus associated with?","choices":["Retinoblastoma and osteosarcoma","Anal, vulvar, vaginal, penile and oropharyngeal cancer","Hepatocellular carcinoma and gastric adenocarcinoma","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma and certain B cell lymphomas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — all five are listed alongside cervical cancer, the oropharyngeal one being a squamous cell carcinoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which inherited defect underlies xeroderma pigmentosum, and what is the consequence?","choices":["Defective apoptosis genes, so damaged cells cannot self destruct; increased risk of lymphoma","Defective protooncogenes, so growth signalling is lost; increased risk of aplasia","Defective repair genes, so ultraviolet B damage cannot be repaired","Defective tumour suppressor genes, so cell growth cannot be inhibited; increased risk of nervous system tumours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the consequence is an increased risk of skin cancer in sun-exposed areas. It is a repair-gene disease rather than a tumour suppressor one. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What is metaplasia, and what provokes it?","choices":["Enlargement of an organ because its individual cells enlarge under increased demand","Autonomous growth of an abnormal mass of tissue derived from a single genetically altered cell","Change of one cell type to another under chronic irritation or injury","Disordered growth of epithelial cells with variation in size and shape and loss of architectural orientation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it involves a change in differentiation. The example on the slide is bladder transitional epithelium becoming squamous. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How did Professor Rappa explain skeletal muscle hypertrophy?","choices":["Striated muscle cells do not divide, so each fibre gets larger instead","Resistance training recruits satellite cells, so the number of muscle fibres increases","Protein intake causes fluid retention within the muscle, so the organ appears larger","Resistance training converts slow fibres to fast fibres, changing the tissue type"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — protein and resistance build more actin and myosin within the existing cells. Bigger fibres, not more fibres, which is the whole distinction from hyperplasia. — 2026-08-20 lecture recording, 2:54"},{"q":"What is a malignant tumour of mesenchymal origin called?","choices":["A papilloma","A sarcoma","A carcinoma","An adenoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — mesenchymal means supportive tissue, and the malignant form is the sarcoma. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What happens when a protooncogene mutates?","choices":["It becomes a tumour suppressor gene, which then inhibits cell growth excessively","It becomes a repair gene, which then introduces errors into the genome","It becomes silent, so the cell loses its ability to grow at all","It becomes an oncogene, which encodes oncoproteins that promote continued uncontrolled growth"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the same machinery, with the brake removed. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Where does the gene damage in carcinogenesis come from?","choices":["It arises only from errors during deoxyribonucleic acid replication","Inherited, and/or acquired from chemicals, radiation or microbes","It is always inherited through the germline and never acquired during life","It is always acquired during life and never inherited"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the named sources are chemical carcinogens, ultraviolet and ionizing radiation, and microbial organisms: viruses and one bacterium. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What are the steps of haematogenous spread, in order?","choices":["Cells separate and degrade intercellular tissue with enzymes, invade the vessel, then travel","Tumour cells adhere more tightly to each other; the mass compresses a vessel; a single fragment travels to one other organ","Tumour cells enter lymphatic vessels at the margin and follow the natural route of drainage","Tumour invades through an organ surface into a body cavity and settles on the serosal membranes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — multiple tumour fragments travel to other organs, so one organ may end up with several metastatic nodules. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"On the deck's dysplasia diagram, what separates severe dysplasia from invasive cancer?","choices":["The basement membrane","The degree of nuclear enlargement, which is greater in invasive cancer","The presence of mitoses, which appear only once invasion has occurred","Involvement of the regional lymph nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — carcinoma in situ sits above it and invasive cancer has broken through. The diagram runs normal, mild, moderate, severe, carcinoma in situ, then invasive cancer. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which chemical carcinogens are classically emphasised in occupational bladder cancer?","choices":["Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons","Alkylating agents","Asbestos fibres","Aromatic amines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons come from tobacco combustion. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What kind of organism is Helicobacter pylori, and what does it cause in the stomach?","choices":["A virus that integrates its genome into gastric epithelial cells","A fungus that colonizes the gastric mucosa in immunosuppressed patients","A Gram-negative bacterium causing chronic gastritis","A Gram-positive bacterium that colonizes the stomach, causing acute ulceration and perforation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it colonizes the stomach and may lead to atrophic gastritis and intestinal metaplasia. It is the one bacterium among the microbial causes in this deck. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does anaplasia mean?","choices":["Close resemblance to the comparable normal cell","Failure of a primordial tissue to develop into a mature organ","Disordered growth of epithelium with retained architecture","Lack of differentiation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the extreme of the grading scale, with no resemblance to the normal cell. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What do promoters do?","choices":["They cause the permanent deoxyribonucleic acid damage that starts the process","They repair the deoxyribonucleic acid damage caused by initiators","They cause tumour cells to detach and enter the circulation","They drive proliferation of cells already damaged by an initiator"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — sustained or enhanced proliferation raises the risk of successive mutations leading to cancer. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What are mesenchymal tumours derived from, and what tissues does that include?","choices":["Neural tissue: peripheral nerves, nerve sheaths and the central nervous system","Supportive tissue: connective tissue, adipose tissue, cartilage, smooth and striated muscle, and bone","Epithelial surfaces: skin, gastrointestinal lining, respiratory lining and glandular ducts","Haematopoietic tissue: bone marrow, lymph nodes, spleen and thymus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the same origin gives sarcomas when the tumour is malignant. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What do initiators do in chemical carcinogenesis?","choices":["They repair damage already caused by another carcinogen","They trigger apoptosis in cells with damaged genomes","They cause permanent damage to deoxyribonucleic acid","They cause sustained or enhanced proliferation of already-damaged cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — permanence is what distinguishes initiation from promotion. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Is metastatic spread random?","choices":["No — it is determined by blood flow, receptors, and genetically determined fitness","Yes — circulating cells lodge in whichever capillary bed they reach first","Yes for haematogenous spread, no for lymphatic spread","No — but only because lymphatic drainage is anatomically fixed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the slide makes this the headline. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Who did Professor Rappa say is the only one who can diagnose cancer?","choices":["The pathologist","The radiologist","The surgeon who obtains the specimen","The oncologist who stages the disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — he said it twice. Grading is a histological judgement made on tissue. — 2026-08-20 lecture recording, 22:36"},{"q":"Which three concepts does the hepatitis B slide emphasise?","choices":["Pleomorphism, abnormal nuclei, and abnormal differentiation","Self-renewal, immortality, and differentiation capacity","Chronic inflammation, regenerative hyperplasia, genomic instability","Initiation, promotion, and progression"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the same triad also explains hepatitis C and Helicobacter pylori. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What do the prefixes leio- and rhabdo- mean in a sarcoma's name?","choices":["Leio- is bone; rhabdo- is cartilage","Leio- is glandular; rhabdo- is squamous","Leio- is smooth muscle; rhabdo- is skeletal muscle","Leio- is skeletal muscle; rhabdo- is smooth muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and adeno- means glandular, while the suffix -oma simply means tumour. — 2026-08-20 lecture recording, 14:43"},{"q":"Why does the deck say genes promoting self destruction of cells with damaged deoxyribonucleic acid are necessary?","choices":["To limit the total number of cells an organ can contain","To supply nutrients to neighbouring cells as they proliferate","To slow the rate at which stem cells commit to differentiation","To stop the damage becoming permanent in dividing cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — apoptosis prevents a mutation being carried on into the daughter cells. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What do Nx, N0 and N1 to N2 or higher each mean?","choices":["Nx, nodes removed surgically; N0, nodes present but normal in size; N1 upwards, enlarged nodes","Cannot be assessed; none; increasing number and range involved","Nx, no regional nodal metastasis; N0, nodes cannot be assessed; N1 upwards, distant metastasis","Nx, nodes not yet sampled; N0, one node involved; N1 upwards, two or more nodes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — x always means cannot be assessed, 0 always means none found, and the numbers rise with the number and range of nodes involved. — Abnormal Cell Growth for posting.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A sample from an oesophageal carcinoma shows COLUMNAR cells. What did Professor Rappa say that implies?","choices":["It is a benign adenoma rather than a carcinoma","It is secondary to reflux disease","It is a primary oesophageal cancer","It is a metastasis from a gastrointestinal primary"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — squamous cells would indicate a primary cancer, since the lower oesophagus is normally squamous. — 2026-08-20 lecture recording, 8:47"}]};
